## Results

Both runs started the guest and reached `retired=2,999,999,998`,
`budget_exhausted`. Run 0001's strict nonce marker was fused with the next
printk and failed validation; run 0002's marker was valid. The automatic series
therefore ended `outcome=failed`, exit code 1, `matches=false`, with unexplained
`run[1].scenario_status`, `run[1].marker_validation`,
`run[1].machine_observations`, and
`run[1].ordered_uart_bytes differs after nonce normalization`.

| Run | Marker status | #PF | #GP(0) | PM timer reads | Halt idle ns | Virtual ns | Quanta | IRQ0 edges / delivered |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 0001 | invalid: fused | 34 | 1 | 230 | 1,902,227 | 37,473,362,225 | 34,580,429 | 34,816 / 34,821 |
| 0002 | valid | 34 | 1 | 230 | 1,823,440 | 37,448,686,438 | 34,556,348 | 34,791 / 34,796 |

Run 0001 contains the single UART line
`OD_NONCE=OD518e83b7f6953264800000d7e00000000[    2.271144] Run /init as init process`.
Run 0002 prints `[    2.271112] Run /init as init process` before its intact
nonce line. Thus this N=2 pair again observes both the fused failure and the
reverse order of two otherwise complete lines. The earlier byte-context
singles localize these lines to distinct serial writer paths but do not prove
the interleaving cause.

## Virtual-time accounting

The runner's board clock adds one nanosecond per retired instruction, one
microsecond per successful PM timer read, one microsecond per successful PIT
time-port read (`0x61` or `0x42`), one microsecond per guest RDTSC, and explicit
halt idle time. After subtracting `retired`, `halt_idle_ns`, and
`pm_timer_reads × 1,000 ns`, the residuals are:

- Run 0001: `34,471,230,000 ns`.
- Run 0002: `34,446,633,000 ns`.
- Difference: `24,597,000 ns` = 24,597 combined RDTSC/PIT time-port ticks.

Both runs have exactly 230 PM timer reads, so PM timer activity contributes
230,000 ns per run and cannot explain this difference. The remaining counts
are not currently distinguished between RDTSC and PIT port reads. Quanta
differ by 24,081 and IRQ0 edges/deliveries by 25. These correlations do not
establish whether one source or guest scheduling caused the variation. Do not
normalize any of these values.

